Breaks: A terminal command leaves its name held after it exits. The process check frees it again, so what is left is a stale holder in the file and a window where a reused pid makes a command that finished minutes ago look like a live chat — which is the one thing peers is not allowed to infer.
Must fail: go test ./internal/cli -run '^TestATerminalCommandWillNotTakeAnOpenChatsName$' -count=1

